#: Game.Levels.SetWorld
msgid "# **Sets & Set Connectives**\n"
"A **set** §0 is a collection of objects of some type §1 that satisfy a **membership predicate**, say §2. We often write sets by explicitly referring to the membership predicate. We call this **set-builder** notation. Here's how we would write the set of even integers in set-builder notation:\n"
"| | | |\n"
"|---|:---:|---|\n"
"| | ![Set-builder notation illustration](images/set1.png) | |\n"
">\n"
"Often we omit the §3 (so the above might look like §4) if the type of objects in the set is understood. Set **connectives** (like intersection, union, etc) are also defined by *membership predicates*. Let's see how it all works!"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `left`
#. §1: `⊢ P ∨ Q`
#. §2: `left`
#. §3: `⊢ P`
#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.arg_docs
#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.arg_docs
msgid "The §0 tactic is useful for targeting goals that are disjunctions. Specifically, if our goal is of the form §1, the tactic §2 will make our new goal §3."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `x ∈ univ`
#. §1: `True`
#. §2: `x ∈ univ`
#. §3: ```
#. rw[mem_univ_iff_true]
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L13_set
msgid "The membership proposition §0 means the same thing as §1. 👉 To rewrite §2 in this form, try\n"
"§3\n"
""
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.QuantifierWorld.QuantifierWorld_eg.L02_quant
msgid "The converse of this is also true."
msgstr ""

#. §0: $$ (P → Q) ↔ (¬ P ∨ Q) $$
#. §1: `Imp_iff_not_or`
#. §2: $$
#. \\begin{array}{|cc||cc|}
#.   \\hline
#.   P & Q & P → Q & ¬ P ∨ Q  \\
#.   \\hline
#.   T & T & T & T \\
#.   T & F & F & F \\
#.   F & T & T & T \\
#.   F & F & T & T \\
#.   \\hline
#. \\end{array}
#. $$
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.equiv_docs
msgid "This theorem is the so-called **Conditional Law**. It says that\n"
"\n"
"§0\n"
"The proof of §1 follows from the truth-table below:\n"
"§2"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.L04_arg
